  "observations": [
    "Follower and server counts agree for every route and outcome; fixture requests in the eviction cohort are listed separately.",
    "Large prefixes multiply polling: 60.3 polls per lifecycle (56.0 pending) at 100 MiB against 10.0 (5.0) at 1 MiB with matched output; polls per committed MiB 34.3 against 5.7.",
    "Server proof compute averaged 854 ms per 100 MiB proof (about 8.5 ms per prefix MiB); three growing 100 MiB followers used 10.2 s of compute in 32 s.",
    "Every periodic sweep of a growing session rehashes the full prefix locally (663.5 MiB per 100 MiB lifecycle) and on the server.",
    "Restart, a 301 s server clock advance and sixteen later proofs each produced one verification_unknown per follower with reasons unknown_since_start, expired and evicted; each was recovered once with one extra submission, one or two polls and one renewed local prefix hash.",
    "A quiet follower beside three live peers lost its reused proof to eviction once in 65 s (1 of 9 polls) while the peers admitted 24 proofs.",
    "All receipts verified, no transient retries, no dropped telemetry events."
  ],
  "dispositions": {
    "pendingProofPolls": "Dominant avoidable traffic for large prefixes. Candidate for the MCP prefix project: space polls by expected compute or a server hint, conditional on a matched rerun showing fewer polls without delaying resume or finite receipts by more than one interval.",
    "periodicFullPrefixProofs": "Server and local cost grows with prefix size for growing sessions. Candidate: scale the periodic interval with prefix size while keeping bounded sweeps, conditional on matched evidence.",
    "reusedProofLookups": "Cheap per poll, but reused results become unknown at the five-minute terminal lifetime or under eviction churn. Candidate: submit a fresh operation before the lifetime ends; whether an unchanged verified boundary needs a remote lookup is an API/MCP decision question.",
    "appendsHeartbeatsMetadata": "Proportional to output, idle time and lifecycle starts; required for commit latency and liveness. No change.",
    "missRecovery": "Bounded at four consecutive losses; one extra submission and one local rehash per loss. Preserve."
  },
  "limitations": [
    "One host and loopback HTTP; no network shaping, packet loss or multi-host routing.",
    "Lifecycles are 34 to 66 s; steady-state expiry rates (about one miss per 31 reuse polls for a quiet follower) are projected from code constants, not measured over hours.",
    "Restart is modelled by routing to a second server application with an empty registry sharing the database, not by restarting the native server.",
    "Server compute uses the registry clock from admission to the terminal result and includes queueing behind the two workers.",
    "The 10 November sample (1,464 polling 404s in 30,422 polls) cannot be re-attributed without its server logs; future logs carry the reason."
  ]
}
